class:: SpecBandwidth
summary:: General p order spectral bandwidth
categories:: UGens>Analysis>MIR
related:: Guides/MIR2, Classes/SpecCentroid2

description::
Returns (sum(w * abs(f - centroid).pow(p)) / sum(w)).pow(1/p), in Hz. The centroid uses the same weights. With p = 2 this equals FFTSpread2.

For these formulas w is the raw FFT magnitude or its square, and f is the bin-center frequency in Hz. Silence returns 0. The reader preserves the FFT chain. Peeters (2004), A Large Set of Audio Features for Sound Description; mathematical definitions are implemented independently. Source: link::https://recherche.ircam.fr/anasyn/peeters/ARTICLES/Peeters_2003_cuidadoaudiofeatures.pdf::.

CPU cost is linear in the number of FFT bins; no memory is allocated while processing. It adds no lookahead beyond the upstream FFT. At a 1024-sample window and 48 kHz the initial analysis window is about 21.3 ms. Changing FFT size or window affects unnormalized values.

Like vanilla link::Classes/SpecCentroid::, this reads FFT frames and produces control-rate timbre information; this class measures general p order spectral bandwidth instead of the average frequency.

classmethods::
method:: kr

argument:: chain
FFT chain from an FFT with the same literal fftSize. New-frame values update the output; between frames the previous output is retained.

argument:: p
Order, clipped to 0.25..8. Default 2 is standard deviation.

argument:: minFreq
Lowest included bin center in Hz; default 0 includes DC.

argument:: maxFreq
Highest included bin center in Hz; 0 selects Nyquist. Empty ranges return 0.

argument:: spectrumMode
Literal 0 uses magnitude; literal 1 uses squared magnitude (power).

argument:: fftSize
Literal power of two from 256 through 8192. Must match the FFT buffer.

returns::
One control-rate descriptor value.

examples::
SpecBandwidth calculates the generalized p-th order spectral bandwidth in Hz around the spectral centroid. Narrow bandwidth corresponds to focused pure solo tones, while wide bandwidth indicates breathiness, multi-pitch textures, or noise. In this example, spectral bandwidth modulates the Q factor of a resonant formant filter, narrowing into sharp resonance on pure tones and widening gently during noisier breath segments. An interactive code::MIR2GUI.scope:: displays real-time spectral bandwidth in Hz.
code::
(
Routine({
    var path = MIR2GUI.soundPath("melody.wav");
    ~mir2Buffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~mir2Bus = Bus.control(s, 1);
    s.sync;
    ~mir2Example = {
        var source = if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) {
            PlayBuf.ar(1, ~mir2Buffer, BufRateScale.kr(~mir2Buffer), loop: 1)
        } {
            // Synthetic melody fallback
            Saw.ar(Select.kr(LFNoise0.kr(2).range(0, 5), [220, 261.63, 293.66, 329.63, 392, 440])) * 0.2
        };
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var chain = FFT(LocalBuf(1024), source, 0.25);
        var bw = SpecBandwidth.kr(chain, p: 2, minFreq: 50, maxFreq: 15000, spectrumMode: 0, fftSize: 1024);
        var resQ = (bw / 3000).clip(0.1, 1.0).lag(0.08);
        var filtered = BPF.ar(source, 1500, resQ) * 0.4;
        var sound = (source * 0.25) + filtered;

        if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { Out.kr(~mir2Bus, bw.clip(50, 8000)) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2) ! 2
    }.play;

    ~mir2GUI = MIR2GUI.scope("Spectral Bandwidth", ~mir2Bus, 100.0, 4000.0, "Hz");
}).play(AppClock);
)
::
code::
(
~mir2Example.free;
if(~mir2GUI.notNil) { ~mir2GUI.close };
if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { ~mir2Bus.free };
if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) { ~mir2Buffer.free };
)
::

